# firewall5: NAT masquerade, how your home router works
#
#     a 10.1.0.2 --- r0 10.1.0.1 [r] r1 203.0.113.1 --- 203.0.113.2 b ("the internet")
#
# a has private addressing; b (a web server "on the internet") has no route
# back to 10.1.0.0/24, exactly like real internet hosts know nothing about
# your 192.168.x.x. So r must rewrite the source address of packets leaving
# towards b to its own public address (source NAT). `masquerade` is SNAT to
# "whatever address the outgoing interface has". Connection tracking maps
# the replies back.
#
# The check loads this on r and runs, in a:  curl http://203.0.113.2/whoami
# which reports the client address b saw. It must be 203.0.113.1.
#
# Your task: this masquerades on the wrong interface.

flush ruleset

table ip nat {
  chain postrouting {
    type nat hook postrouting priority srcnat; policy accept;
    oifname "r0" masquerade
  }
}
